WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.490
Our water tank sensor works perfectly.

2
00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:05.930
But the tank is up a hill, four hundred

3
00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:08.450
metres from the office, and our Wi-Fi

4
00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:10.070
stops at the staffroom door.

5
00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.540
So nobody knows when it's nearly empty.

6
00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.220
Today: networks, and how a

7
00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:18.540
sensor's data gets home.

8
00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:21.420
Every network is a trade-off,

9
00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.600
so ask four questions.

10
00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:24.950
How far?

11
00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:26.660
How much power?

12
00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:29.340
How much data, and how often?

13
00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:31.570
And what will it cost each month?

14
00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:35.250
Bluetooth Low Energy reaches ten

15
00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:37.490
to thirty metres, usually to a

16
00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:39.270
phone, on very little power.

17
00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:42.860
Wi-Fi carries lots of data, but only

18
00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:45.620
thirty to fifty metres indoors, and

19
00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:46.980
needs a router with power.

20
00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:49.890
A mobile network works wherever

21
00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:52.010
there's coverage, but each sensor

22
00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:54.050
needs a SIM and a monthly plan.

23
00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:57.730
LoRa is made for sensors: two to five

24
00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:00.470
kilometres in town, ten or more in open

25
00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:03.770
country, on very little power, but only

26
00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:05.470
a few dozen bytes at a time.

27
00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:08.630
Sometimes the best network

28
00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:10.530
is no radio at all.

29
00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.960
A logger saves to a memory card, and a

30
00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:17.080
pupil collects it weekly: no signal, no

31
00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:17.260
bill.

32
00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:18.960
Quick check!

33
00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.540
The tank is four hundred metres away, up

34
00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:25.200
a hill, with no power or Wi-Fi.

35
00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:27.000
Which network fits best?

36
00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:28.810
LoRa.

37
00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:32.000
Four hundred metres is easy for LoRa,

38
00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:34.360
but far beyond Bluetooth and Wi-Fi.

39
00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:37.590
A water level is a few bytes, the sensor

40
00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:40.510
can run for years on batteries, and one

41
00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:42.770
gateway can hear sensors all over the

42
00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:43.030
school.

43
00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:46.800
A gateway is the bridge between the

44
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.720
sensors' radio and the internet.

45
00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:51.930
It hears their messages and passes them

46
00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:55.230
on, over Wi-Fi, a cable or the mobile

47
00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:57.530
network, to a computer or a phone.

48
00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:00.640
What if a sensor is out of reach?

49
00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:04.840
In a mesh, sensors pass messages hop by

50
00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.100
hop, each to a neighbour in range,

51
00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:09.160
until one reaches the gateway.

52
00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:11.780
If one fails, the message can

53
00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:12.780
often go another way.

54
00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:14.450
Your turn.

55
00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:17.500
A sensor at the far end of the garden

56
00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.360
can't reach the gateway, but a sensor

57
00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:22.080
halfway can reach both.

58
00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:24.430
What's the cheapest fix?

59
00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:28.060
Let the halfway sensor pass the messages

60
00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:30.940
on: in a mesh, they hop to the gateway.

61
00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:34.900
Remember: weigh distance, power, data

62
00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:38.180
and cost; Bluetooth or Wi-Fi when it's

63
00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:41.060
near, LoRa and a gateway when it's far,

64
00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:42.960
and hops to cross the gaps.

65
00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:46.350
Try it in class tomorrow: the

66
00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:47.630
playground radio game.

67
00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:51.830
One pupil is the gateway, and notes pass

68
00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:54.390
only to someone within arm's reach.

69
00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:56.620
Who gets cut off?

70
00:02:57.380 --> 00:03:00.240
Add a relay, then take one pupil

71
00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.540
away and find another route.
